<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Chanka Confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1438 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of highland peoples whose attack on Cusco, according to Inca tradition, triggered the rise of the Inca Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chanka, or Chanca, occupied the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apurimac-region">Apurímac</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wari-empire">Wari Empire</a>. From around 1100 they lived in fortified hilltop settlements and formed a loose confederation of chiefdoms based around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andahuaylas">Andahuaylas</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Archaeology shows small fortified villages on high ridges rather than a centralised state, suggesting a warlike society of local chiefs who joined forces for war. They herded llamas and alpacas and farmed tubers. Chanka groups claimed descent from the lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-choclococha">Choclococha</a> and worshipped mountain spirits. The Chanka are remembered chiefly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> tradition, which portrayed them as fierce rivals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> accounts, around 1438 the Chanka besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Cusi Yupanqui</a> defeated them, took the name Pachacuti and began the expansion that created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. Historians now treat this story partly as Inca propaganda. The Chanka were absorbed into the empire.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1100 CE – 1438 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44